Hypothesis (1) must say that ρ is minimal up to twist, which is all that Theorem 1.4 can give. The fix: first twist ρ̄ by a character ε̄, and ρ by its Teichmüller lift, so that ρ̄^{I_v} ≠ 0 whenever ρ̄|G_v is ramified and reducible (§3.1). Then require, for every prime v:
(a) if ρ̄ is unramified at v, then ρ is unramified at v;
(b) if ρ̄^{I_v} = 0, then ρ(I_v) ⥲ ρ̄(I_v);
(c) if ρ̄^{I_v} is a line, then rank ρ^{I_v} = 1 and det ρ|I_v = ⟨det ρ̄⟩|I_v.
Put ψ = det ρ·⟨det ρ̄⟩^{-1}. This character has finite order, being unramified at p. Under (a)–(c), ρ⊗ψ^{-1/2} is minimal in the sense of Def. 3.1, so Theorem 1.4 applies and twisting back gives the corollary. Equivalently, simply assume that some finite-order twist of ρ is a minimal deformation of the correspondingly twisted ρ̄.
The correction proposed in X-K1-0 is right, given that it twists ρ̄ first as it states. The correction in H-part1a-12, which adds only the determinant condition, is not enough, because it misses the unipotent-reduction case (ii) below.

Replace "Since π1^* is fully faithful, the claim follows" with a direct moduli computation on all of H^0(X_0(x), ω). Take g on X_0(x) with points (E, α_N, C), and let φ_D : E → E/D be the quotient map. The paper's formulas are:
- W_x g(E,α,C) = φ_C^* g(E/C, φ_Cα, E[x]/C) (p. 315);
- tr(π1)h(E,α) = Σ_D h(E,α,D);
